  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nA dry cold front will move through this morning. Cannot rule out\na rogue shower in the Alleghenies, but the frontal passage is\nexpected to largely be moisture starved. Winds will shift to \nthe W and NW at 10 to 15 mph with gusts of 20 to 25 mph, \nperhaps occasional/peak gusts near 30 mph near higher terrain. \nDespite the passage of the front, downsloping winds likely \npushing temps well into the 70s to near 80 for most. Lowered Tds\ngiven the downsloping setup NBM was not catching at all.\n\nSeasonable (albeit slightly cooler) temps and dry conditions \nwill then persist under high pressure Fri through Fri\nnight. Lowered Tds in aft given recent NBM issues and favorable\nsetup for lower Tds.\n\nHigh pressure retreats off to the NE Sat with cloudcover from\nthe remnants of Isaias moving in and thickening through the day.\nAny precip likely holds off until Sat night or Sun morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias arrive late this \nweekend.\n\nTropical Storm Isaias continues to be the main weather feature to \nmonitor over the forecast period with remnants expected to impact \nthe forecast area later this weekend. The National Hurricane Center \nhas TS Isaias tracking a bit slower and further east than previous \nforecasts, with remnant circulation over central Kentucky early \nSun afternoon. Dry conditions are expected for the forecast \narea through Sat, with precip associated with remnants of \nIsaias reaching the forecast area on Sun. The most likely \nimpact to the forecast area continues to be rain although severe\nweather cannot be ruled out on Sunday. Most likely rainfall is\nan inch or two, but still highly uncertain. This many days out,\nthe track, timing, and impacts of Tropical Storm Isaias remain \nuncertain so be sure to monitor hurricanes.gov for the latest \nforecast.",
